  "strategy": "Approach and rationale\nI'll use a radial basis function optimization approach with adaptive time management. My strategy is centered around treating the sequence as samples from Gaussian and sinc-related kernels, which naturally fit the optimization problem structure due to their decay properties. I'll:\n1. Implement an optimized Gaussian-like sequence generation\n2. Use analytical differentiation on the normalized sequence space (sum=1)\n3. Set up a local optimization framework with built-in restarts\n4. Apply disciplined gradient descent with smoothing \n\nThis differs significantly from the existing LP-based approach by focusing on the mathematical structure of the solution space and using proper numerical optimization techniques rather than fallback methods.\n\nMathematical specification\nWe'll work with normalized sequences (sum=1) clamped to [0,1000]. The objective to minimize is \\( \\frac{2n}{m} \\cdot \\max(\\text{convolve}(a,a)) \\), where \\( m \\) is the sum. We'll use:\n- Gaussian kernel approximation to model decay\n- Sinc-like functions for their smooth convolution properties\n- Projected gradient updates in the positive orthant\n\nImplementation procedure\n1. Read runtime variables:\n   - budget_s: Total time limit in seconds\n   - logmagnitude: Given (1025 in example)\n   - maxmagnitude: Given (1999)\n\n2. Set up helper functions:\n   - Normalized convolution with analytical peaks\n   - Streamlined Euclidean projection to simplex\n   - Double-sided smoothing kernel generation\n\n3. Choose primary sequence lengths to test:\n   start_n = min(512, max(128, max(logmagnitude-200, 0)))\n   Then test around: start_n, start_n*1.2, start_n*0.8 (if applicable)\n\n4. Initialize sequences using:\n   - Normalized Gaussian: \\( \\exp(-\\frac{|i-j|^2}{2\\sigma^2}) \\)\n   - Sinc truncation: \\( \\frac{\\sin(\\pi i / n^2)}{\\pi i / n^2} \\)\n   - Smart interpolation from parent if available\n\n5. Perform iterative optimization:\n   - Start with candidate sequence and evaluate objective\n   - Use analytical gradients for faster convergence\n   - Apply coordinate-wise parameterization for trigonometric sequences\n\n6. Termination conditions:\n   - No improvement in objective for 20 consecutive steps\n   - Exceeded time limit (1800 seconds)\n   - Fails the Simpson rule post-optimization constraint\n\nSearch choices and useful variations\nThe algorithm integrates:\n- Primary optimization over normalized Gaussian sequences\n- Global search with pacing by gradually increasing n\n- Analytical termination of returns with gradient validation\n\nComputation budget and robustness\nTime breakdown:\n1. Beam sequence generation: 100-150 seconds\n2. Initialization of Gaussian variants: 30 seconds\n3. Main optimization loop: 2/3 of budget time\n4. Backup evaluations if main loop stalls\n\nThe set of key lengths to test is: [128, 256, 512, 1024] with priority on ones near the given thresholds.\n\nValidation and return contract\nThe algorithm explicitly:\n1. Maintains a clamp of [0, min(maxmagnitude, 1000)]\n2. Performs gradient smoothing at each backlog\n3. Uses a 4-phase testing approach with increasing rigor\n4. Returns the best candidate found by objective evaluation",
